{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_25","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,220p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1583749884/6/codegen_prompts/1.md\"","aggregated_output":"你要为一个新的 ARC-AGI 风格题目实现代码。代码文件必须包含 `main(grid: np.ndarray) -> np.ndarray` 和 `generate_input() -> np.ndarray`。\n\n## 新题位置\n\n- 描述目录：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed1583749884/6`\n- 题号：`1`\n- 目标代码路径：`/home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/code/combo_new/seed1583749884/6/1.py`\n\n## 新题描述\n\n<new_puzzle index=\"1\">\n- `description`：将顶部指令条中的标记颜色用作过滤器，保留下方场景中匹配颜色的对象，并将每种标记颜色的对象数量编码为指令条中的水平柱状图。\n\n- `Input structure`：\n  - 背景为 Black。\n  - 网格最上方有一行指令条（高度为 1-2 行），条内有若干个彩色标记像素（如 Red、Blue、Green），其余为背景色。\n  - 指令条下方是一个矩形场景面板，面板背景为 Grey，面板内散布着若干 4-连通的单色前景对象，对象颜色包括标记条中出现的颜色和未出现的颜色。\n\n- `Rule`：\n  - 在场景面板中，保留颜色与指令条标记匹配的对象，将所有其他前景对象重着色为面板背景色 Grey。\n  - 在指令条中，对每个标记颜色，将其标记像素替换为一个从最左列开始的水平色条，色条长度等于场景中该颜色存活对象的数量。\n  - 如果某标记颜色在场景中无对应对象，则该标记像素被清除为背景色。\n\n- `Key insight`：指令条同时起过滤和计数展示两个作用：标记颜色决定哪些对象保留，而保留的对象数量又回填到指令条中作为柱状图。\n</new_puzzle>\n\n## 机制相似参考题\n\n下面参考题由本地相似度流程自动选择：先用 `annotations`/docstring/代码特征抽取机制标签，再按机制相似度、文本相似度和代码特征相似度重排，并用 MMR 做去重。优先学习这些题的对象识别方式、随机生成器约束、边界条件处理，不要照搬旧题规则。\n\n已选参考：eval/247ef758, train/abbfd121, eval/62593bfd, train/84551f4c, train/e3fe1151, train/3cd86f4f, train/6d1d5c90, train/902510d5\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"1\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"247ef758\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3452\nmechanism=0.4525\nlexical=0.0162\ncode_feature=0.2857\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, object:marker, object:rectangle, object:sprite, operation:count, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/247ef758.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/247ef758.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nMove colored sprites from the left strip into a framed area at marker-defined targets.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg background; rectangular frame on right with colored markers on its edges; sprites in a narrow left strip separated by a constant-color divider column.\n\nRule:\n- For each color, target rows/cols are where both opposite frame edges share that color. Targets are the Cartesian product.\n- Erase each sprite from the left and stamp it centered at every target. Higher sprites cover lower ones.\n\nKey insight: One sprite replicates to a grid of row × column marker intersections.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_to_match\",\n  \"src\": \"sprite s in the left area whose color appears on the right frame\",\n  \"dst\": \"center at positions d in the right frame\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"s.color == d.same_row_frame_edges.color and s.color == d.same_col_frame_edges.color\",\n  \"multi_step\": \"move sprites from bottom to top, with new covering old\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"abbfd121\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3085\nmechanism=0.3986\nlexical=0.0159\ncode_feature=0.2857\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, object:rectangle, object:sprite, operation:shape_match, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/abbfd121.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/abbfd121.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nRecover the repeating background pattern hidden under the largest solid rectangle.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid is covered by a small row/column periodic pattern.\n- Several non-pattern colors form solid rectangular foreground objects that\n  overwrite the pattern.\n- The requested output region is the largest of those solid rectangles.\n\nRule:\n- Find every 4-connected monochromatic object whose bounding box is a filled\n  rectangle with both dimensions greater than one.\n- Treat those rectangles as masks, infer the smallest repeating tile that is\n  consistent with all remaining visible pixels, and repaint the largest\n  rectangle's coordinates with that tile.\n\nKey insight: The rectangle colors are occluders; the visible pixels outside\nthem are enough to reconstruct the original periodic background underneath.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"symmetry/pattern\",\n  \"src\": \"visible periodic background outside the solid rectangular occluders\",\n  \"dst\": \"region covered by the largest solid rectangular occluder\",\n  \"transform\": \"infer the minimal repeating tile from unoccluded pixels and output the tile values that would occupy the largest rectangle's coordinates\",\n  \"grid_shape\": \"same shape as the largest rectangle\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"3\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"62593bfd\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.3143\nmechanism=0.3399\nlexical=0.0160\ncode_feature=0.6667\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:counter, code:rotation, object:sprite, operation:count, operation:shape_match, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/62593bfd.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/62593bfd.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nSort objects to top or bottom edge by whether their shape (up to rotation/flip) is shared.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Several multicolor connected components (8-connected) scattered across the grid.\n\nRule:\n- Compute each object's canonical shape (binary mask, invariant to rotation and flip).\n- Objects whose shape appears in two or more components → translate to TOP edge (row 0).\n- Objects whose shape is unique → translate to BOTTOM edge (row H−h).\n- Horizontal position (left column) is preserved; only vertical translation changes.\n\nKey insight: The sorting criterion is shape frequency (rotation-invariant), not area — duplicated shapes go up, unique shapes go down.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"move_in_order\",\n  \"src\": \"all connected components (objects) on the background\",\n  \"dst\": \"translate each object d vertically to an edge (x preserved)\",\n  \"match_cond\": \"group objects by their pixel-count (area). If an area value occurs >=2 times, those objects go to the TOP edge; if the area is unique, that object goes to the BOTTOM edge\",\n  \"transform\": \"duplicated-size -> top; singleton-size -> bottom\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"84551f4c\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.2897\nmechanism=0.3311\nlexical=0.0224\ncode_feature=0.5000\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:connected_components, code:rotation, object:marker, operation:count, relation:direction, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/84551f4c.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/84551f4c.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nRotate each blue-started chain of bottom-aligned vertical bars into horizontal bars resting on the same baseline.\nInput structure\n- The grid has a background and bottom-aligned, one-cell-wide vertical bars.\n- Blue bars are markers; red bars are body bars that may be chained after a marker.\nRule\n- Starting at each blue bar, repeatedly include the next red bar whose column is exactly the current bar's column plus the current bar's height.\n- Clear every included vertical bar, then redraw each included bar horizontally to the right from its original bottom cell, with length equal to its vertical height.\n- Red bars that are not reached from a blue marker remain unchanged.\nKey insight\n- A vertical bar's height is also the width it occupies after rotation, so the chain follows immediate horizontal contact after each rotated bar.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"transform_in_place\",\n  \"src\": \"bottom-aligned one-cell-wide vertical bars on the background; blue bars start chains and red bars may follow\",\n  \"transform\": \"for each blue-started chain, rotate each reached vertical bar clockwise onto the shared baseline; the next red bar is reached when its column is exactly the current bar's column plus the current bar's height\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle rank=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e3fe1151\">\n<similarity>\nscore=0.2911\nmechanism=0.3643\nlexical=0.0168\ncode_feature=0.3333\noverlap_tags=code:bbox, code:counter, object:marker, object:rectangle, operation:count, relation:row_col\n</similarity>\n<paths>\ncode=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/e3fe1151.py\nannotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/e3fe1151.json\n</paths>\n<description>\nFill the one blank in each corner region so all four regions have the same color multiset.\n\nInput structure:\n- The dominant background color forms one solid separator row and one solid\n  separator column.\n- The separator cross splits the grid into four equal rectangular corner regions.\n- Each corner region contains exactly one background-colored blank pixel.\n\nRule:\n- Count the non-background colors in each corner region.\n- The completed region multiset is defined by the maximum observed count of\n  each color across the four regions.\n- Replace each region's blank pixel with the single color needed to match that\n  completed multiset.\n\nKey insight:\n- The separator color inside a corner region is a missing value marker; comparing\n  the four regions reveals the shared multiset without using numeric color order.\n</description>\n<annotation>\n{\n  \"type\": \"transform_in_place\",\n  \"src\": \"four equal corner regions divided by a solid background cross, each with one background-colored blank pixel\",\n  \"transform\": \"fill each blank with the color needed so every corner region has the same color multiset, using the maximum observed count of each color across the regions\"\n}\n</annotation>\n</reference_puzzle>\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
